NAME
       loginctl - look at and control login sessions and users (systemd-logind)

SYNOPSIS
       loginctl [list-sessions]
       loginctl list-users
       loginctl user-status [USER]
       loginctl show-user [-p PROPERTY] [--value] USER
       loginctl enable-linger [USER] | disable-linger [USER]
       loginctl terminate-user USER | kill-user USER

DESCRIPTION
       systemd-logind keeps track of who is logged in: each login (on a
       console, through ssh, in a graphical session) is a session, and each
       user with at least one session gets their own systemd instance
       (user@UID.service) that runs their user services.

       Normally that user instance stops when the user's last session ends,
       and all the user's services stop with it. Lingering changes that: for
       a user with linger enabled, the user instance starts at boot and keeps
       running without any login. That is what a rootless container (or
       any systemctl --user service) needs to start at boot and keep
       running after you log out.

COMMANDS
       list-sessions
              The open sessions: ID, UID, user, seat, terminal (the default).

       list-users
              The users with sessions (or lingering).

       user-status [USER]
              A user's sessions and the processes in them.

       show-user USER
              A user's properties, such as Linger=yes or no and State=.

       enable-linger [USER], disable-linger [USER]
              Turn lingering on or off (for yourself without USER; root for
              others). It is stored as a file in /var/lib/systemd/linger/.

       terminate-user USER
              End all of the user's sessions.

       kill-user USER
              Send a signal to all of the user's processes.

OPTIONS
       -p NAME, --property=NAME
              With show-user, show only this property.

       --value
              Print only the value, without NAME=.

EXAMPLES
       Make student's container service start at boot:

           loginctl enable-linger student
           loginctl show-user student -p Linger

       Who is logged in?

           loginctl list-sessions

       Log a user out everywhere:

           loginctl terminate-user student

NOTES
       Use ssh student@localhost (or a real login) rather than su - student
       to manage systemctl --user services: su does not give the user a login
       session of their own, so systemctl --user may fail to reach the user
       instance.

FILES
       /var/lib/systemd/linger/USER
              Exists while lingering is on for USER.

SEE ALSO
       systemctl(1), systemd-logind(8), podman-generate-systemd(1),
       podman-systemd.unit(5)
